<common-name datatype='string'>putative ethanolamine catabolic microcompartment shell protein EutN</common-name>
<comment datatype='string'>Several genes of the &lt;i&gt;eut&lt;/i&gt; operon are involved in the ability to utilize ethanolamine as the sole source of carbon and nitrogen in &lt;i&gt;Salmonella typhimurium&lt;/i&gt;; the operon is conserved between &lt;i&gt;Salmonella&lt;/i&gt; and &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt;.  EutN has not been shown to be involved in ethanolamine utilization in either &lt;i&gt;Salmonella&lt;/i&gt; or &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt; [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/10464203&quot;&gt;Kofoid99&lt;/a&gt;].  EutN is similar to the carboxysomal shell protein CcmL of &lt;i&gt;Synechococcus&lt;/i&gt; sp. strain PCC7942 [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/8491708&quot;&gt;Price93a&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/10464203&quot;&gt;Kofoid99&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
A crystal structure of EutN has been solved at 2.4 &amp;Aring; resolution.  The protein forms a tight hexamer with a central hole that is predominantly covered by acidic residues, suggesting a pore with a transport function [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/17588214&quot;&gt;Forouhar07&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
EutN: &quot;&lt;b&gt;e&lt;/b&gt;thanolamine &lt;b&gt;ut&lt;/b&gt;ilization&quot;
&lt;p&gt;
CchB: &quot;&lt;b&gt;c&lt;/b&gt;arbon dioxide &lt;b&gt;c&lt;/b&gt;oncentration &lt;b&gt;h&lt;/b&gt;omolog &lt;b&gt;B&lt;/b&gt;&quot; [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/7868611&quot;&gt;Stojiljkovic95a&lt;/a&gt;]</comment>
